  "slug": "same-tally-voucher-number-different-entries",
  "title": "Why the Same Tally Voucher Number Can Point to Different Entries",
  "description": "Use company, type, date and party to identify the intended voucher when an invoice number alone is ambiguous.",
  "category": "Vouchers and Day Book",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Distinguish type from number",
      "body": "A sales voucher and a receipt can display the same number under different numbering sequences. For example, Sales 102 and Receipt 102 are separate transactions. Start by asking which type is being referenced."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Include the company and period",
      "body": "If you manage several companies, open the relevant one before looking up the number. Set the financial year and date range. When a number still appears more than once, compare party and amount with the source document."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Use a complete reference in messages",
      "body": "Instead of sending a colleague only 102, include the company, voucher type, number and date. Add the party when more than one record still matches. This reference helps them trace the same transaction without sending unnecessary financial details."
    }
  ],
